The API map, verified:

```js
// TRAVERSAL
el.closest('.card');           // UP to the nearest matching ancestor
el.parentElement;
el.children;                    // Elements only
el.childNodes;                  // ALL nodes — includes text + comments!

// QUERY DOWN
document.querySelector('.item');
el.querySelectorAll(':scope > li');   // :scope = search from HERE, direct children

// BUILD
const li = document.createElement('li');
li.className = 'item';
li.textContent = userInput;         // SAFE for text — no parsing
list.append(li);                    // also prepend, before, after, replaceWith

// INSERT HTML (parses a string — content must be trusted!)
el.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', markup);

// BATCH appends — one reflow, not N
const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
for (const item of data) frag.append(buildRow(item));
list.append(frag);                  // single DOM entry
```

The cost model (the interview core): DOM WRITES mark layout dirty but batch; layout READS (offsetHeight, getBoundingClientRect, getComputedStyle) FLUSH pending changes to give a correct answer. The thrashing loop — read height, write style, read height again — forces a reflow EVERY cycle; the fix is phase discipline: collect ALL reads, then apply ALL writes. Frameworks do this for you (React batches DOM changes and applies them in one pass — one of the reasons virtual-DOM diffing pays).

The safety line: innerHTML/insertAdjacentHTML PARSE markup — a user string inside becomes live elements (<img onerror=...>). textContent for text, property assignment for attributes that shouldn't parse (a.src, not a.setAttribute with a crafted value), <template> for authored static markup, and sanitize any user HTML explicitly.
